faq:
  - q: "How do I use LinkedIn to find a job?"
    a: "LinkedIn serves two job search functions: (1) Passive — optimize your profile so recruiters find you inbound. Headline with exact target role keywords, About with outcomes and targeting signal, Skills at 50, Open to Work in recruiter-only mode. This generates inbound without any active search. (2) Active — use LinkedIn to find and reach out to recruiters, hiring managers, and contacts at target companies; apply to roles within 48 hours of posting; use LinkedIn Sales Navigator or search to identify who is hiring."
  - q: "Does LinkedIn Easy Apply actually work?"
    a: "LinkedIn Easy Apply has a significantly lower conversion rate than direct application through a company's careers page. Recruiters can see the source of applications — and Easy Apply signals lower intent than a direct application. Use LinkedIn to identify the role, then apply directly on the company website. Reserve Easy Apply only when a direct application option does not exist."
  - q: "How do I turn on Open to Work on LinkedIn without my employer seeing?"
    a: "Go to your LinkedIn profile → 'Open to' → 'Finding a new job.' Select 'Recruiters only' — this hides the Open to Work green badge from your public profile and from your current employer's employees. LinkedIn does not guarantee 100% privacy, but this setting dramatically reduces visibility to your current company. Avoid 'All LinkedIn members' if you are in a confidential search."
  - q: "How often should I post on LinkedIn while job searching?"
    a: "Posting 1–2 times per week significantly increases profile visibility and recruiter impressions. Posts that perform well for job seekers: insights from your work (frameworks, lessons, observations), takes on industry trends, questions that invite engagement from your target audience. Posting is optional — an unoptimized profile with frequent posts is still less effective than an optimized profile with no posts. Fix the profile first."
  - q: "Should I message hiring managers directly on LinkedIn?"
    a: "Yes — with a specific, brief message. Identify the hiring manager for a role you have applied for or are interested in. Message: '[Name], I just applied for [Role] at [Company]. I'm genuinely interested — particularly in [specific aspect of the role or company]. I wanted to reach out directly to express my interest and offer to connect if that would be helpful.' Do not send this message without context; a cold message with no specificity is ignored."
